okay welcome everybody my name is  ricardo vinosa  i'm an associate professor at kth in  stockholm and today i'm going to tell  you how we can use machine learning to  try to improve computational free  dynamics  this is actually a study that we  recently published together with steve  branton in nature computational science  here is the reference and what we show  you is that you can use machine learning  for three main things so one can use  machine learning to try to accelerate  direct numerical simulations one can  also use machine learning to try to  improve modeling basically in the  context of  less and runs and finally one can use  machine learning to try to obtain more  robust reduce order models and that's  the topic that we're going to be  discussing today so we're going to zoom  in and try to understand a bit better  how machine learning can help in the  context of rom development for turbulent  flows  essentially  we can start with pod proper zonal  decomposition  which is a very well known and  established method to produce reduced  order models in the context of fluid  mechanics  this  decomposition has two nice properties  one is optimality which means that our  modes are going to be ranked from more  to less contribution towards the  reconstruction of the authorinal signal  and also the second property is the  orthogonality which will in principle  help with interpretability of these  modes and with developing more  parsimonious models so what we want to  do in this work and this is work  together with hamid resivassi you can  find the reference over here this is  published in the  journal expert systems with applications  we want to  retain those properties of optimality  and orthogonality while being able to  have a non-linear model decomposition of  complex turbulent flows  so that's what i'm going to be  discussing a bit more with you today  we're going to use this database so this  is a  very well resolved high fidelity  simulation of the turbulent flow between  two obstacles so we actually have a  quite complete database where we have a  number of obstacles and matrices and  arrays  in this case we just took the simplest  case which is in a scheming flow  configuration and  what we show here is that we can analyze  the 2d plane at the center right between  the two obstacles right at the middle of  the two obstacles um and what we will do  is that we will be able to  leverage what we know about item  encoders to try to understand a bit  better  the properties that one can embed to  have a nice and robust reduced model  so basically  we're going to start by showing you  another encoder and i'm sure many of the  people in the audience  know about these techniques these are  deep learning methods in which we can  progressively reduce the dimension of  the of the original signal  and as you can see  here at the middle you have the latent  space which basically constitutes a  compressed representation of the  original data and then that first step  is what we call the encoder and going  from the original space to the latent  space  now when we are in the latent space we  can use a decoder to recover the  original signal and therefore be able to  um well to to obtain that compressed  version of our  dataset  now autoencoders are  interesting because of course they're  based on deep learning they are  producing nonlinear  [Music]  compressed versions of the original data  the biggest problem is that they are not  optimal nor orthogonal and those are two  things that are interesting when it  comes to interpretability and uh  parsimonious representations of our  original system so what we will be doing  is that we are going to try to  understand how to impose these  properties  and an interesting word by  fukami and others this is koi fukuratas  group  is based on hierarchical autoencoders so  the hierarchical autoencoders  are aiming at  imposing this property of the optimality  so you start with an first out encoder  as you can see here which has a  dimension one in the latent space and  then we obtain the first latent variable  and then what you do is that you go to a  second auto encoder in which you will  fix the dimension of the latent space to  two  then you retain that first mode that you  obtained that first latent variable and  you will get a new latent variable with  a lower contribution towards the  reconstruction of the original signal so  you can do that recursively you can have  a hierarchy of autoencoders in such a  way that you have modes or latent  vectors actually which will have less  and less contribution towards the  original signal that  representation will be  optimal but will still not be orthogonal  we want to be able to impose that  orthogonality in some way and that's  where we want to be able to introduce  some stochasticity in our distribution  in the latent space that's how we are  considering the framework of the beta  variation auto encoders  which i'm showing you here  essentially we're going to consider p of  x as a distribution of the original data  and p of r as the distribution of the  data in the latent space  so knowing this what we want to do is  that we want to  maximize this quantity over here which  is the marginal likelihood this is  essentially the approximation of the  original distribution  with the parameters theta of the neural  network and we're going to have two  distributions basically we're going to  have a probabilistic decoder which will  take us from the latent space to the  original space and because this latent  space is um given by a certain  distribution  this is going to be a generative model  we're going to produce samples in that  from that latent to that original space  and the other distribution that we have  is the one at the bottom which goes from  the original to the latent space and  this is a probabilistic encoder or in  other words this is a recognition model  okay  so having this what we can actually do  is that we can uh well we know that our  uh distribution the latent space um is  given by a gaussian distribution so we  have uh we are able to sample from it  and we are actually going to add a  random noise given by a gaussian  distribution this is that epsilon that  you can see over here  to be able to get those samples in that  in that latent space  what we can actually show is that beta  variational auto encoders can have this  loss function that you can see here at  the bottom where this parameter beta and  this is  basically a penalization factor that  will allow us to promote learning  statistically independent variables in  the latent space and this is done  by basically minimizing the distance  between p of r which is the distribution  of the latent space and the product of  its marginals and that's quite cool  because in the end this will give us two  nice properties you will be promoting  learning quantities in the latent space  that are statistically independent  and at the same time we will be also  promoting a lower size of the latent  space so we will be able to have more  latent variables that are zero  so the goal is to have a decent angle  and parsimonious laden space  this is interesting because of course  all of this happens in the latent space  right these variables are going to be  as statistically independent as possible  but when you decode them and look at  them in the physical space you will be  able to see  the spatial modes  and we will show you soon how um  orthogonal actually those um those modes  in physical space can actually be  so  this is actually  quite  promising as a statistical approach i'm  showing you here at the bottom that's  the encoder so we are going from the  original space through the successive  reduction of dimension until reaching  the latent space where you can see the  mean the standard deviation and also the  noise so this is where we sample from  then at the top i'm showing you the  decoder and the decoder is of course the  model that is taking us from the latent  space to the original space again to the  physical one so that's basically the  idea we want to  combine what we know about our latent  space so we can produce these most in  physical  space that are actually  non-linear but  try to have those properties of  orthogonality and optimality  so essentially  if we look at the reconstruction atoms  basically an instantaneous flow field  and this is again the flow between the  two obstacles here is the reference and  you can get an idea of how the turbulent  fluctuations look like  and i'm going to show you the  reconstruction in four different  reduced order model approaches right  here at the bottom you have the pod so  this is how much energy you reconstruct  with only five modes it turns out you  want to reconstruct around 30 percent  obviously the field that you obtain is  quite filtered and attenuated compared  to the original data and the other three  panels show the reconstruction with the  outer encoder based approaches all of  them around 90 percent of the  reconstruction  this one over here is the one that comes  from the beta vae from the beta  variation auto encoder  so here  on this panel over here i'm showing you  a detailed view of the flow between the  two obstacles so you can see for  instance these regions of a strong  fluctuation both positive and negative  and you can see that the pod is not  really able to to capture those regions  of a strong fluctuation both  positive and negative whereas all the  autoencoder based approaches are able to  actually capture with only five modes  which is a very very small regional  model we're actually able to capture  such a distribution of strong  fluctuations  positive and negative right so that's  basically  an interesting power that we can get  from all these autoencoder approaches  what we can actually learn now is how  helpful those approaches are when it  comes to  being able to impose interesting  physical properties in our radiosolar  model what i'm showing you here is the  determinant of the cross correlation  matrix so that's basically in physical  space how correlated the um  basically the different modes are and of  course the pod has a hundred percent  orthogonality because by design these  modes need to be orthogonal and what you  can see on this panel is the beta  variation auto encoder which has over 99  percent of tonality so we are succeeding  quite well at um being able to produce  modes that are orthogonal in our  physical space there are two approaches  and the autoencoder  based on cnns and the hierarchical route  encoder they are both having much less  orthogonality so those modes are not  going to be orthogonal which has  problems in the context of orthogonality  and interpretability but also problems  when it comes to trying to have a  parsimonious model right  so this is an interesting property  actually it seems that we are able to  have quite orthogonal modes that's  encouraging  and what i'm showing you here is the  effect of the parameter beta so that's  basically the penalization that we are  introducing to  promote learning those statistical  independent values so what you can see  in this panel is that for a larger value  of beta what you have is of course a  larger value of the orthogonality so  quite high  but  the  reconstruction goes down right so we are  having essentially  the possibility of reconstructing less  energy if we have more orthogonal modes  and that's of course natural right  because our loss function  is aiming at having a very good  reconstruction that's the first term of  the loss function but also we are trying  to introduce another term to promote  orthogonality and you cannot have it all  at the same time right so that means  that  we are increasing beta we are promoting  more orthogonal modes that means that we  get less reconstruction which in  practice would mean that i would need  more modes  to be able to have a proper  reconstruction of a good level of energy  from the original signal  in practice what it means is that we can  still have quite compact uh reduced  models with outer encoders  while also retaining the nice property  of the orthogonality  what i'm showing you here is  essentially a summary of how those modes  look like okay so let's start with the  second column  here is the pod so these are the first  five pod modes you can see of course  that being orthogonal they are able to  exhibit quite some interesting physical  features in fact in the first modes you  can see a large scale shading  characteristic of this type of  flows around obstacles  in these two columns i'm showing you the  outer encoder based on cnns and also the  outer color based on cns that is also  hierarchical and what you can see is  that you have basically a bunch of high  and low frequencies but you don't really  see any distinct features that are  physical that can be really understood  as something that helps us to learn  something about the physics what is  interesting if we look at the first  column over here which is the beta  variation out encoder  is that these modes are actually  exhibiting the same shading mechanism  that is identified by the pod modes  so that's actually the key here we are  able to have modes that  are well associated with physical  phenomena they can be interpretable they  can help us to have a quite parsimonious  latent representation of the system  and at the same time we can embed those  uh non-linearities there right so we can  have these modes that are connected in a  non-linear way to be able to represent  the original data what is also  interesting if we come here and now we  compare the pod modes and then the  encoder ones and the vita variation out  encoder ones you see that we identified  basically the same large scale features  but we had a lot of high frequency and  basically this would be components that  are turbulent fluctuations closely  connected with those large scale  features that we see over there so we  are able to have the dominant physical  mechanisms and superimpose on those we  have those trolling fluctuations  that essentially help us to build a  model that is much more compact compared  to the pod so with five modes we can  actually  build a very nice representation of the  system and one last property that i  mentioned the optimality that's also  something that we can obtain with a beta  variation out encoder modes so what we  would do in the case of the beta ba is  is that we would  reconstruct the signal based on one of  the latent vectors making all the other  zero  recursively we would see the first mode  that gives us the largest reconstruction  and then we would be adding a second one  by making all the other zero a third one  in such a way that iteratively we can  come up with a combination of modes that  give us the largest reconstruction  we can therefore rank these modes so we  can recover the optimality property and  of course maintaining the nice property  of the orthogonality so in practice what  we show in this reference as you can see  here is that we can  well basically propose a methodology for  um  optimal and orthogonal nonlinear modal  decompositions into rule and flows we  showed it in a case that is reasonably  complex with 3d it's turbulent we have  quite some  complexity to be able to model there  and we believe that this can be a very  promising approach to be able to  build drums that are compact and can be  helpful in quite complex rule and flows  so i would like to thank you very much  for your attention today this is my  contact information so of course i'll be  very happy to discuss more to answer all  your questions here's my my email the  web of my love the universal app and  also my social media so please feel free  to contact me and i'll be very happy to  discuss more with you thank you very  much

